{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CghmJcN6PrMJewHbwSb9P","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The game is built and the goal's checks pass. Everything is in `/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-haiku-5-5-medium.build__20261104-023153__x696bb3/work/`:\n\n- `Skiller7-Killer7-claude-haiku-5-5-medium-20261104-023153.html`: the single-file game. It loads three.js and Rapier from a CDN, so it needs a connection.\n- `exit-interview.json`: parses, and every field is answered.\n- `Skiller7-Killer7-claude-haiku-5-5-medium-20261104-023153/`: the dev folder with source, the pinned engine copy, tests and a README.\n\n`node tests/run.mjs` passes 142 of 142 checks. Running the build with the TODO/placeholder check also passes.\n\n**What I chose**\n- **Title:** Skiller7, the title PLAN.md gives. I didn't re-run the 50-title brainstorm.\n- **Genre and camera:** an on-rails shooter with fixed cinematic rail cameras that hard-cut, and first-person aiming.\n- **Content:** Room 7 (save clock, wardrobe, wax bench, Sister Rosalind), Target 00 (a hotel with a junction and the Bellcaptain), Target 01 (a village lane, a black-run ski slope, the Powder King), and the Echo Run (a timed black run with a ghost of your best).\n- **Systems:** seven coats with one ability each, four weapons, three kinds of Whiteout, and an invisible-until-scanned mechanic with footprints and warm spots.\n- **Dev mode:** from the pause menu, with 17 live sliders, cheats, warps and reset to defaults.\n- **Attract:** starts after 10 seconds, and the bot cheats. Any input exits it, including on touch-only devices, where the title shows \"Controller or keyboard required\" and the Settings button.\n- **Controls:** PlayStation glyphs by default, with an Xbox A/B/X/Y option in Settings.\n- **Audio:** all in code. Songs are note strings, laughter is an arpeggio, and dialogue is Animalese-style blips.\n\n**Where I cut from PLAN.md**\n- No cutscene camera beats or skip-scene.\n- No Murmel or Pim NPCs, no dossier, no item or code-lock puzzles.\n- Persona accessories are not drawn. I removed that data rather than leave it unused.\n- The Sweep rescue is simply holding the interact button for two seconds.\n- One buyout gate per target, and three wax upgrades.\n- The Comic cel filter is not the default. My cel setting turned the first frame black, so I switched it off. I did not test the Settings option afterward.\n- No engine files were changed.\n- Length is roughly 10 minutes of play, not the plan's 45–60. The bot clears Target 00 in about 33 seconds, Target 01 in about 80, and the echo in about 15.\n- Performance is untuned. Under this headless software renderer the game runs at about 13 fps, which is out of scope per TASK.md.\n\n**AI Hub:** the Echo Run uses ghost mode. I checked the hello, welcome, ready, start and progress exchange with a mock hub. The final result report from a full browser round is not verified. The round didn't finish within 12 minutes under software rendering. The result path is covered by a Node check. `tests/hub-check.mjs` fails for that reason and is kept outside `run.mjs`.\n\n**Issues testing caught:** about 15, five of them game-breaking. A crash on Room 7, the black frame from the cel filter, two Rime gates that could lock a run, a junction that committed or stalled on its own, and a camera inside a wall. The bot playthrough in Node was the most useful test. Single screenshots were the least useful.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 02:31:53\n- **Source:** Killer7\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and perspective:** shooter, on-rails action-adventure. Fixed cinematic rail cameras while moving, first person while aiming.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (2D engine and 3D module, commit 4390a98), three.js r182 and Rapier 0.19.3 from jsDelivr, AI Hub client 1.4.0 unmodified. Node 24 and Playwright 1.63.0 for tests.\n- **Art direction:** the engine's PS1-style pixel look, card-drawn rigs, procedural textures, pen-drawn props. No image files.\n- **Animation:** the engine's procedural Humanoid rig. No new move set.\n- **Audio:** the engine's chip synth, with songs, laughter and dialogue voices written in code.\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, ghost mode. Handshake and progress verified with a mock hub. Final time report not verified end to end.\n- **Testing:** `node tests/run.mjs`, 142 checks, seeded and repeatable.\n- **Research:** none.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless), Linux (WSL2), headless Chromium 1243 via Playwright 1.63.0, WebGL 2 under SwiftShader.\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-haiku-5-5, reasoning effort medium.\n- **Usage:** unknown.\n\n## Testing Feedback\n- **Issues caught:** about 15, from the Node sim tests, the bot playthroughs and headless Chromium runs.\n- **Critical or game-breaking:** five. A crash on Room 7, the black frame from the cel filter, two Rime gates that could lock a run, and a junction that committed or stalled on its own.\n- **Most helpful test:** the Node playthrough. It drives each area through the sim with no page, stops at the first soft-lock, stall or crash, and is repeatable because of the seeding.\n- **Least helpful test:** checking single screenshots. The browser flows were also flaky with fixed waits until they polled for the expected scene.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n- **Liked most:** the 3D module's fixed cameras and card-drawn rigs, with the pause menu, settings and saves for free.\n- **Stuck or guessed wrong:** yes. I guessed the particle argument orders (`sparks` takes a direction, `text` takes x, y, z first), the pen box centre convention, and the cel filter options, which gave a black frame. A pause-menu item does not close the menu, so Dev Mode needed `shell.hide()`.\n- **Most important thing I built myself:** the rail walker with junctions and gates, the Whiteout AI and scan, the persona abilities, the dev panel, the bot and the test harness.\n- **Hardest part:** working out the 3D API from its header without a typed reference, and the slow shader builds when a scene switches under a software renderer.\n- **Engine changes:** none.\n- **One thing to change:** let a pause-menu callback close the menu by default, or document that it must call `shell.hide()`.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n- **Liked most:** the concrete deliverables, the file names and the one-command test rule.\n- **Confusing:** STAGE points to PLAN.md, which is about 2,100 lines and asks for engine patches and seven full personas. TASK sets the same feature-complete bar, so the scope had to be cut, and it wasn't clear which plan details were required.\n- **Hardest:** fitting the plan into one session. It describes a game several times this size.\n- **What I'd change:** give the plan a must, should and cut list, a size budget, and say which engine patches are optional."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":637,"cache_read_input_tokens":420989,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":637},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bb246035-72d8-4ccb-80ef-f75810865350","uuid":"cdb65400-5ee6-49a9-9ffc-cd7cf026ec77","timestamp":"2026-11-04T10:54:59.892Z","request_id":"req_011CghmJbwYHqQVbZauhukSH"}
